          "id": 337541,
          "format": "MC",
          "difficulty": 1,
          "exampleId": 6618,
          "text": "<p>Consider the following differential equation.\n\\[\ny'' + y' - 12y = 14xe^{-4x}\n\\]\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\\[\ny(x) = A e^{-4x} + B e^{3x} + g(x),\n\\]\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$</p>\n<p><em>Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constants omitted):</em>\n\\[\n\\int x e^{-7x} \\,\\textrm{d}x = - \\dfrac{1}{49}(7x + 1)e^{-7x}\n\\]</p>\n",
          "choice1": " $- \\left( x^2 + \\dfrac{3x}{7} \\right) e^{-4x}$",
          "choice2": " $- \\left( x^2 + \\dfrac{2x}{5} \\right) e^{-4x}$",
          "choice3": "$- \\left( x^2 + \\dfrac{2x}{7} \\right) e^{-4x}$",
          "choice4": " $- \\left( x^2 + \\dfrac{x}{3} \\right) e^{-4x}$",
          "choice5": " $- \\left( x^2 + \\dfrac{x}{7} \\right) e^{-4x}$",
          "freeAnswerChoice": " ",
          "number": 1,
