          "format": "MC",
          "difficulty": 1,
          "exampleId": 6618,
          "text": "<p>Consider the following differential equation.\n\\[\ny''+3y'+2y= (2x-2)e^{-2x}\n\\]\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\\[\ny(x) = A e^{-2x} + B e^{-x} + g(x),\n\\]\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$</p>\n<p><em>Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constants omitted):</em>\n\\[\n\\int  (2x-2) e^{-x} \\,\\textrm d x =-2xe^{-x}\n\\]</p>\n",
          "choice1": "$ - 2x^3 e^{-2x}$",
          "choice2": "$ - x^3 e^{-2x}$",
